    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-36064-6",
    "abstract": "The rhizome of Smilax glabra Roxb., known as Smilacis glabrae Rhizoma, is extensively used in Traditional Chinese Medicine for relieving dampness and other health issues. The growth patterns and geographical distribution of S. glabra are significantly influenced by ecological conditions. This study used the maximum entropy (MaxEnt) modeling approach in combination with the geographic information system (GIS) software (ArcMap) to evaluate and predict the influence of climate change on the geographical distribution of S. glabra. The potential suitable habitats under two greenhouse gas emission scenarios (SSP126 and SSP370) were forecast for two future timeframes (2041-2060 and 2061-2080) by analyzing species occurrence records and environmental factors. Jackknife method was employed to assess the significance of environmental variables, and those contributing \u2265 1.0% to the model were considered as major driving factors. S. glabra is widely distributed in the southern provincial regions of the Yangtze River Basin in China. Currently, the suitable habitat covers 1,755,900 km<sup>2</sup>. In the predicted future climate scenarios, the suitable habitat area is projected to extend to a maximum of 1,915,400 km<sup>2</sup>. The distribution of S. glabra is significantly influenced by rainfall, daily temperature variation, and soil clay composition. Moreover, under both the projected climate scenarios, the core of suitable habitats for S. glabra were predicted to migrate southward. Overall, our findings offer a scientific basis for developing conservation approaches and optimize their spatial distribution management of the genetic sources of S. glabra.",
    "field": "Species distribution modeling",
    "problem": "The decline of wild *Smilax glabra* resources due to over-harvesting and ecological shifts necessitates predicting its future habitat suitability under climate change to guide conservation, as its distribution is significantly influenced by specific environmental conditions.",
    "method": "This study uses the maximum entropy (MaxEnt) model combined with geographic information system (GIS) software to analyze environmental variables and predict the future habitat suitability and geographical distribution of *Smilax glabra* under various climate change scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to predict the future habitat suitability and geographical distribution of the medicinal plant *Smilax glabra* in China under different climate change scenarios using an ecological niche model.",
    "model": [
        "BCC-CSM2-MR"
    ],
