        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MaxEnt model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "AUC",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The MaxEnt model results were evaluated the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves. In the ROC curves, x-axis corresponds to 1-specificity, which quantifies the false positive rate (FPR) or the likelihood of erroneously predicting the presence of a species in areas where it is truly absent. The y-axis represents sensitivity or the true positive rate (TPR), which reflects correct predictions of the presence of a species in actual distribution areas33. Model performance was evaluated using the area under the curve (AUC) value, which represents the probability of ranking a randomly chosen positive instance higher than a randomly chosen negative instance by the model.\nMaxEnt model for S. glabra achieved an AUC value of 0.935 (Figure 2) and a TSS of 0.779 for the current period (Table 2). Furthermore, future projections (2041-2060 and 2061-2080) for the SSP126 and SSP370 scenarios also demonstrated mean AUC values above 0.9 and TSS values above 0.7 (Table 2).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MaxEnt model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "TSS",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Considering the potential limitations of relying solely on the AUC metric, particularly the risk of overestimation when the background extent (set as the entire territory of China in this study) is large36, we further introduced the True Skill Statistic (TSS) for comprehensive evaluation. The TSS ranges from -1 to 1, with higher values indicating better predictive performance of the model. TSS measures a model's ability to distinguish suitable from unsuitable habitats by comprehensively considering both sensitivity and specificity, calculated as TSS = sensitivity+ specificity-1. This metric is insensitive to class imbalance problems and is therefore commonly used in species distribution modeling as a supplementary evaluation tool alongside the ROC curve37.\nMaxEnt model for S. glabra achieved an AUC value of 0.935 (Figure 2) and a TSS of 0.779 for the current period (Table 2). Furthermore, future projections (2041-2060 and 2061-2080) for the SSP126 and SSP370 scenarios also demonstrated mean AUC values above 0.9 and TSS values above 0.7 (Table 2).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MaxEnt model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "omission rate",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Additionally, we calculated the omission rate for the test dataset. This calculation was based on the logistic threshold of maximum training sensitivity plus specificity, which is a threshold that aims to balance the model's ability to discriminate between presence and background points39.\nAdditionally, under the current climatic scenario, the MaxEnt model demonstrates an omission rate of 6.58% on the test set, with close agreement between the omission rates of the test and training samples and the predicted omission rate (Supplementary Figure 1).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "BCC-CSM2-MR",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "higher resolution in both atmospheric and land-surface processes",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "Finally, this study relies on future climate projections from a single global climate model (GCM), BCC-CSM2-MR. Although BCC-CSM2-MR offers higher resolution in both atmospheric and land-surface processes, provides finer topographic representation, and demonstrates better performance in simulating orographic precipitation and local temperature distribution60, differences exist among GCMs.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
